// tsx/next-01: source order for JavaScript and TypeScript, while core's `earlier` has no rows for them.
//
// What a rule gets from this library:
//   src_before(m, n)        m starts before n in the same document (by start offset)
//
// core.rerail says earlier(m, n) is start-offset order outside Python, but the engine fills visit_index, which
// earlier is lowered to, for Python documents only (facts/vtab/syntax/tree.rs), so on a .js or .tsx file every
// `earlier` is false and every `!earlier` true. src_before is that documented order, read off node's offsets.
// An ancestor and its first descendant start at the same offset, so neither is src_before the other; the rules
// here compare statements, siblings, or an assignment target and a later read, which never nest.
// When the engine gives earlier rows for these documents, this file goes and its rules say `earlier` again.
//
// Builds on core.

.decl src_before(m: node, n: node)
src_before(m, n) :- node(d, m, _, a, _), node(d, n, _, b, _), a < b.

// Examples, one per rule that uses this library (packs/next):
//   no-async-client-component      "use client"; export default async function Page() {}       "use client" first
//   no-duplicate-head              <Html><Head /><Head /></Html>                                  the first Head
//   no-location-assign-relative-destination  let u = "https://x"; u = "/a"; location.href = u    the last write
